// Practice quiz: Records
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/records

::o1 generated members::A record Point declares components x and y. Which members does Java provide without you writing them? {
~Getters getX() and getY()#Not quite. Record accessors are named after the components, without get.
~Setters for x and y#Not quite. Records have no setters; their fields are final.
~Only toString#Not quite. Constructor, accessors, equals and hashCode are provided as well.
=A canonical constructor, accessors x() and y(), equals, hashCode and toString#Correct. The record header determines them all.
}

::o1 equals::Two Point records are created with the same x and y. What does equals return? {
=true#Correct. Record equals compares the component values.
~false, because they are different objects#Not quite. That is the default for ordinary classes, not records.
~It does not compile#Not quite. equals is provided automatically.
~true only if both came from the same constructor call#Not quite. Equal component values are enough.
}

::o2 final fields::Why can code not assign p.x after creating a record p? {
=Record fields are private and final, so they are set once in the constructor#Correct. Records are shallowly immutable carriers of data.
~x is a static field#Not quite. Record component fields are instance fields.
~Records hide every field from their own methods#Not quite. The record's own methods can read the fields.
~You can, through a generated setX#Not quite. Records generate no setters.
}

::o2 when a record fits::Which type is the best fit for a record? {
~A BankAccount whose balance changes over time#Not quite. Its state changes, so a class fits better.
=A value such as a Money amount with a currency, compared by its contents#Correct. Records suit plain data whose identity is its values.
~A class that must extend another class#Not quite. Records cannot extend classes.
~An object that needs extra instance fields beyond its components#Not quite. Records cannot declare extra instance fields.
}

::o3 compact constructor::What is a compact canonical constructor for? {
~Adding extra components#Not quite. Components are fixed by the header.
~Making the fields mutable#Not quite. They stay final.
=Checking or normalizing component values before the fields are assigned#Correct. You write the checks; Java assigns the fields at the end.
~Replacing equals and hashCode#Not quite. Those are separate methods.
}

::o3 reject bad input::A compact constructor finds that lo is greater than hi. What should it usually do? {
~Assign this.lo directly#Not quite. Fields cannot be assigned in a compact constructor; Java assigns them afterwards.
=Throw an IllegalArgumentException#Correct. Throwing stops the invalid record from ever being created.
~Return null#Not quite. Constructors cannot return a value.
~Print a warning and continue#Not quite. The invalid record would still be created.
}
